 "preamble": "Because they regard it as a trifle, not worthy of consideration, critics, editors and biographers of Shakespeare never mention that he compiled a crossword (published here for the first time) or that he wrote the following extract from The Winter’s Tale (Act III, sc. iii) primarily to serve as its clues across. These clues consist of one word in each line and since they were written in or around 1610 solvers should be on the alert for words and meanings now obsolete. Anxious to get on with other work, the Bard never compiled the clues down; so Dogop has supplied normal ones. Shakespeare did however leave a note that the unchecked letters of his across lights form the unchline: ‘GONERIL THE SORRY THRIVER? YES!’—WINTER’S TALE (BARD) and, to avoid pitfalls, solvers are urged to check these letters with those in their completed diagrams.",
